The '''Constitution of 1812''' was the basic legal framework adopted in [[Sarta]] after the end of the [[Second Sartan Civil War]]. It standardised the kingdom's bureaucracy, regularised tax codes, and created a system of municipalities and provinces. The constitution also reduced the king's authority and placed government more firmly under the control of a unicameral parliament dominated by lords.

== Background ==

The constitution was drafted in a period of caution and reconstruction. Sartan politics after 1811 were shaped by the desire to avoid another civil war, but also by pressure to stabilise administration and clarify the relationship between the crown, local government, and the nobility. Support for recovering the Volisanian-held coast remained strong, yet most political leaders preferred negotiation and institutional reform over renewed conflict.

== Provisions ==

The Constitution of 1812 formalised a more uniform state structure. Municipalities became the basic local units of civil administration, while provinces provided a broader regional layer for taxation, record-keeping, and oversight. These reforms made the kingdom's internal boundaries more regular and gave the central government a more reliable administrative system.

The constitution also limited the king's powers. The crown remained the symbol of the kingdom and retained some personal sway, but real authority was placed under a unicameral parliament controlled by lords. The result was an oligarchic constitutional order in which the monarchy continued to exist, but could no longer govern without the cooperation of the landed elite.

== Political effect ==

In practice, the constitution strengthened the bargaining power of large landowners and regional nobles. It encouraged the formation of a bureaucratic state, but it did not democratise Sartan politics. The political order remained cautious, restricted, and highly sensitive to signs of division, especially in the years when debate over Volisanian territory remained unresolved.

Later reforms built on this framework, including the [[Act on Free Movement of 1838]], which abolished serfdom and compensated the lords at considerable cost to the treasury.

The constitution became the legal foundation for the later territorial and administrative settlement of the kingdom, including the provincial structure that followed the [[Occidental Agreement of 1846]].

